WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.556
Use mission and asset identifiers in file names and folders.

00:00:05.556 --> 00:00:12.078
Organize originals, working products, annotations, maps, defect records, reports, and exports separately.

00:00:12.078 --> 00:00:18.117
Preserve the relationship between every reported observation and its source file.

00:00:18.117 --> 00:00:25.606
Metadata may include time, position, aircraft, sensor, camera settings, altitude estimate, and processing history.

00:00:25.606 --> 00:00:34.060
Preserve it where appropriate, but validate location after navigation anomalies and avoid treating metadata as infallible.

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:42.032
Inspection notes should describe asset component, observation, evidence reference, environmental context, confidence, and recommended follow-up.

00:00:42.032 --> 00:00:50.487
Defect tags organize review but should not imply engineering severity unless a qualified authority assigns it.

00:00:50.487 --> 00:00:57.975
G I S integration can connect observations with approved asset layers and repeat inspections.

00:00:57.975 --> 00:01:03.048
Check coordinate reference, accuracy, asset identifiers, and map currency.

00:01:03.048 --> 00:01:09.087
A precise symbol can still be attached to uncertain source data.

00:01:09.087 --> 00:01:15.126
Reports distinguish direct observation, automated detection, analyst judgment, and specialist conclusion.

00:01:15.126 --> 00:01:23.581
Include overview and close-up evidence, limitations, missing coverage, collection conditions, and what additional inspection is needed.

00:01:23.581 --> 00:01:30.103
Infrastructure data can reveal layout, condition, access routes, vulnerabilities, and operational details.

00:01:30.103 --> 00:01:39.524
Apply access control, encryption and secure transfer where appropriate, backup, retention, controlled sharing, and review before public release.

00:01:39.524 --> 00:01:42.665
Version reports and correction records.

00:01:42.665 --> 00:01:57.400
If a component label, location, interpretation, or severity changes after specialist review, preserve the superseded conclusion and explain the update so downstream teams do not act on stale findings.
